{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If there arise in your midst a prophet.</b> The sense of \"in your midst\" is that there is no prophet except from Israel, and this pericope is joined because of idolatry, for burning of children to it. A prophet who says that he awoke the word of HaShem, or His messenger is with him, or in a dream. Some say it is possible for the prophet to be among those who steal the word of HaShem, and the meaning of \"prophet\" is a prophet of truth, that there be a sign such as this to vindicate him, and the hearer hears and tells, to be a sign for himself. Some say that even if the sign and the wonder come, one is not to believe him, for it is a thing counter to reason. In my view, there is both sign (ot) and wonder (mofet), like sign and witness — the words of Isaiah, who said: \"Behold, I and the children whom YHWH has given me are for signs and for wonders\" (Isaiah 8:18), and the sign of the prophet was when Isaiah My servant went naked and barefoot, and the signs of his sons were their names, concerning the event that would occur in their days — and they are Immanuel, Maher-shalal-hash-baz, Shear-jashub — and in the plagues: \"and they shall be to you for a sign and for a wonder\" (Exodus 4:21), and similarly R[abbi]."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Resolved the abbreviation רבי׳ at the end as 'R[abbi]' without expanding further, as the referent is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:23:21.800628888Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}